# Derived Query Methods ⭐⭐⭐⭐☆

Spring generates SQL from method names.

Example

```java
findByName(String name)

findByEmail(String email)

findByPriceGreaterThan(double price)

findByStatus(String status)
```

Spring parses the method name and creates the appropriate query.

---

## Example

```java
List<Product>

findByCategory(String category);
```

Internally becomes something like:

```sql
SELECT *

FROM products

WHERE category = ?
```

---

# JPQL ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Sometimes method names become too complex.

Use JPQL.

---

## What is JPQL?

Java Persistence Query Language.

Unlike SQL,

JPQL works with **Entities**, not tables.

Example

```java
@Query("""

SELECT p

FROM Product p

WHERE p.price > :price

""")

List<Product> findProducts(double price);
```

Notice

```
Product

price
```

instead of

```
products

product_price
```

JPQL uses Java class names and field names.

---

# Native SQL

Sometimes database-specific queries are needed.

```java
@Query(

value = "SELECT * FROM products",

nativeQuery = true)

List<Product> getProducts();
```

---

# JPQL vs Native SQL

| JPQL | Native SQL |
|------|------------|
| Uses Entity names | Uses Table names |
| Database Independent | Database Specific |
| Recommended | Use only when necessary |

Interview Answer:

> Prefer JPQL unless you need database-specific features or complex SQL.

